I have been working on a project to develop a computer prewriting
program since the fall semester of 1990, when I applied for a grant from
the Undergraduate Research Program at Virginia Commonwealth
University.  My goal was to create a computer driven prewriting
heuristic, which would be run on VCU's UNIX local area network, and used
by the English Department for the instruction of prewriting techniques.  

Knowing little about prewriting heuristics in the beginning, I began 
collecting information about prewriting from the most available sources:
professors who taught writing, and essays and other readings on
prewriting.
To get ideas for a heuristic, I surveyed professors who taught
writing, because they write daily and know the needs of
their students.  After collecting the responses to the survey, I
assessed the professors needs, and began researching, reading articles
and books on writing, and discovering the best way to develop and present a
prewriting computer program.  

I realized that I was not prepared to write a complex program 
as was needed to be effective.  Time and resources are also problems I
have encountered, as the development and research continues, and the campus
computers have changed, some parts of the program working, none of the
program being tested.  I have learned that the development of such an
involved project takes much longer than I had anticipated.  

An essay which has helped me a great deal during this project is
"Prewriting and Computing," by James Strickland of Slippery Rock
University.  Strickland outlines what a \fIgood\fR prewriting program
should and should not do.  By offering the user "individuality through
branching capabilities, uniqueness through options not available with
traditional pen and paper,"  a computer can work interactively with the
user and simulate human dialogue (Strickland, Writing on Line. 70).
This type of interaction allows the writer to put together information
he or she already has into concrete knowledge: a solid base to work and
write from.  

An effective program is one that is both used and easily usable. 
Strickland emphasizes that if the program immitates an exercise
done with pen and paper, then there is no point in putting it together,
since the writer would probably prefer to use conventional methods,
rather than new and unfamiliar methods of doing the same exercise (70?).

